WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.273 --> 00:00:02.913
When a trash can gets full and&nbsp;
there’s a government shutdown,

00:00:03.792 --> 00:00:06.769
people don’t stop throwing stuff on top of it.

00:00:06.769 --> 00:00:10.160
But I did manage to pick up a couple&nbsp;
truckloads of trash before I was told:

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.520
“Don’t do it anymore.”

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.469
People were not even able to volunteer&nbsp;
during the government shutdown.

00:00:15.469 --> 00:00:17.916
"Stop the shutdown,
stop the shutdown!"

00:00:17.916 --> 00:00:23.120
The US is the only country&nbsp;in the world where 
the government can actually shut down.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.505
And the threat looms nearly every year.

00:00:25.505 --> 00:00:26.880
"Seven days until shut down—"

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:27.520
"Four days—"

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:28.560
"T-minus six days—"

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:29.440
"Five days—"

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:31.681
"Government shutdown at midnight tonight."

00:00:31.681 --> 00:00:35.840
I just feel my gut in my&nbsp;
chest - like, ugh, not again.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.280
So, why does the US even shut down?

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.066
And what happens when it does?

00:00:42.066 --> 00:00:47.186
“You travel 3,500 miles to America&nbsp;
and find that they shut down!”

00:00:49.793 --> 00:00:52.560
Every government in the world&nbsp;
has to do the same thing:

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.440
decide how to spend the country’s money.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.080
In the US, they do that by passing spending bills,

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.242
called appropriations bills,

00:01:00.242 --> 00:01:03.367
that give these federal agencies their budgets.

00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:06.726
It happens every year — or every fiscal year.

00:01:06.726 --> 00:01:08.465
Japan is April 1st.

00:01:08.465 --> 00:01:10.320
In Kenya, it's July 1st.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.880
In the US, it’s October 1st.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:15.200
And if the government misses that deadline...

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.720
The budget wasn't passed.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:17.600
We have no money.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.520
And then, "Oh, you have to come to work anyway."

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.265
Just not getting paid.

00:01:21.265 --> 00:01:23.553
We did get back pay, but, still,&nbsp;
you have to wait for that.

00:01:23.553 --> 00:01:28.480
The saddest thing I’ve ever seen was seeing&nbsp;
all these hard working people in a line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.400
for their food bank.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.537
It’s the conversation at the&nbsp;
dinner table every single night.

00:01:33.537 --> 00:01:35.600
“Well, Dad, do you know when&nbsp;
you’re gonna get paid again?"

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.236
No. I don’t.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.265
It’s the way the US government&nbsp;
was set up. Kind of.

00:01:41.265 --> 00:01:44.880
The answer to why we have government&nbsp;
shutdowns actually starts in the Constitution.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:51.280
“No money shall be drawn from the Treasury, but&nbsp;
in consequence of appropriations made by law.”

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:52.320
And so what that really means

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.520
is that before any federal money&nbsp;
can be spent, there has to be

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:58.240
an actual appropriations law allowing it.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.409
But that can be interpreted in different ways

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:03.685
and it has changed over time.

00:02:03.685 --> 00:02:06.480
Back in the 1800’s there were no shutdowns.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:07.840
But there were other problems.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.080
Agencies would routinely&nbsp;
blow through their budgets

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.361
then keep spending

00:02:13.361 --> 00:02:16.560
and would come back to Congress and ask for more.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.906
So Congress passed the Antideficiency Act.

00:02:19.906 --> 00:02:26.599
It tells federal officials that they really,&nbsp;
really can't spend money without an appropriation.

00:02:26.599 --> 00:02:32.320
But that didn’t stop the government from missing&nbsp;
their deadline, and creating long gaps in funding.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:37.360
As recently as the 1970s, there were&nbsp;
plenty of these funding gaps. And yet...

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.000
The agency sort of pretty much just kept going.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.360
It couldn't possibly be that&nbsp;
Congress wanted them to shut down

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.433
if there was no budget bill passed on time, right?

00:02:46.433 --> 00:02:49.440
But what happened was in the early 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.480
the attorney general issued two opinions&nbsp;
that tightened up this interpretation.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.080
The opinions basically said

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.840
no seriously, unless Congress&nbsp;
has passed an appropriations bill

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:01.920
agencies can’t spend any money.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:06.880
Including to employ the&nbsp;
services of their employees.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:11.025
Even though there technically is&nbsp;
money, we have no access to it.

00:03:11.025 --> 00:03:14.880
And since then, the US has&nbsp;
shutdown-shutdown many times.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.240
1996 was the first big one, for three weeks.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.840
And 2019 was the longest in history, so far.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.480
I actually got to work and they was like,

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.673
“We’re all furloughed. 
They shut the building down.”

00:03:26.673 --> 00:03:29.200
I was like, “Oh, the doors&nbsp;
are not opening right now?”

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.861
They were like, “No. 
It's shut down completely.”

00:03:31.861 --> 00:03:34.160
I was like, “What is a furlough? 
What’s going on?”

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:36.497
I couldn’t work. 
I couldn’t go in.

00:03:36.497 --> 00:03:39.079
We weren’t even allowed to check email.

00:03:39.079 --> 00:03:42.802
So I ended up picking up side&nbsp;jobs 
trying to make ends-meet.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.880
These are all the agencies&nbsp;
of the federal government.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:51.120
Here are the ones that actually shutdown in 1996.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.200
And in 2019.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.889
Shutdowns don't have to be&nbsp;
of the whole government.

00:03:55.889 --> 00:04:01.360
So it might just be that one set&nbsp;
of agencies didn't get funded.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:04.480
Congress couldn't reach agreement&nbsp;
with each other and with the president

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:06.320
on whether that set of agencies would get funded.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:08.640
But the rest of the government&nbsp;
was funded perfectly well.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:15.120
In 2019, around 800,000 federal employees&nbsp;
didn’t get a paycheck for 35 days.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.240
But more than half of them&nbsp;
still had to go into work.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.720
Because there are some jobs&nbsp;
that the Attorney General said

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.960
are exempt from stopping completely,

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:25.200
what we call “essential” employees.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.680
So while the Department of&nbsp;
Transportation was shut down,

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:30.000
air traffic controllers still had to work.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.080
I don’t show up with a headset&nbsp;
and just do that job by myself.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:39.797
All the other aviation safety professionals&nbsp;
that assist us and help us on a daily basis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.797 --> 00:04:41.350
are now taken away.

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:45.520
Homeland Security shut down but&nbsp;
TSA workers still had to show up.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:46.800
Without pay.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.169
We’re one of the lowest paid agencies.

00:04:49.169 --> 00:04:52.273
Not receiving a check every two weeks was hard.

00:04:52.273 --> 00:04:55.284
People on the floor were&nbsp;
smiling and greeting everyone,

00:04:55.284 --> 00:04:59.200
but behind closed doors in the break&nbsp;
room we had people breaking down.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:02.405
National Parks lost millions in entry fees.

00:05:02.405 --> 00:05:05.120
Wildfire mitigation projects were delayed.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:08.080
Immigration court hearings — backlogged.

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:12.080
And, there were thousands of contractors&nbsp;
that work with all of these agencies

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:13.680
who were also affected.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:17.360
If you were just working on&nbsp;
a contract with a company,

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:20.624
you did not get back pay. 
At all.

00:05:20.624 --> 00:05:24.800
My son has chronic asthma —&nbsp;
couldn’t afford his medicine.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:26.645
I felt like I was...

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.974
I was disappointed in them 
and it wasn’t even my fault.

00:05:31.474 --> 00:05:35.109
And a shutdown isn’t just&nbsp;
centralized in Washington, DC.

00:05:35.109 --> 00:05:39.527
Only 15 percent of all federal&nbsp;
employees live in that area.

00:05:39.527 --> 00:05:42.960
Which means the economic-impact is country-wide.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:46.896
Shutting down not only&nbsp;
affects the federal workers.

00:05:46.896 --> 00:05:49.440
If people aren’t getting paid,&nbsp;
they’re not going out to eat.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:52.640
There is a severe economic&nbsp;impact 
to something like that,

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:55.200
especially in a town like Huntsville.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:00.337
Overall, the US economy lost $11&nbsp;billion 
during the 2019 shutdown.

00:06:00.337 --> 00:06:03.361
Some of that was regained when&nbsp;
employees received their back pay,

00:06:03.361 --> 00:06:05.302
but the damage was done.

00:06:05.302 --> 00:06:08.419
And the thing is, this doesn't have to happen.

00:06:08.419 --> 00:06:15.574
The legal framework explains why we end up&nbsp;
having to have shutdowns as a matter of law.

00:06:15.574 --> 00:06:20.400
But why we have shutdowns&nbsp;
truly is a matter of politics,

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.520
which is that the more polarized the parties&nbsp;
are and the more divided our government is,

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:30.080
the harder time Congress and the president&nbsp;
have working out a budget agreement.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:33.840
And having divisive politics&nbsp;
isn’t uniquely American.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:38.400
In Belgium, there have been times&nbsp;
where the politics were so bad,

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:40.337
they just didn’t have a government.

00:06:40.337 --> 00:06:42.400
We didn’t really notice any difference.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:45.120
Our daily lives didn’t really change much.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:49.520
I don’t think the government&nbsp;
could really like — stop.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:50.784
Most countries couldn’t.

00:06:50.784 --> 00:06:52.817
In nearly every other country,

00:06:52.817 --> 00:06:56.000
if the government were to fail&nbsp;
to pass a budget by the deadline,

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.920
agencies would just continue&nbsp;
working with last year’s budget.

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:02.353
There’s a push to do that in the US, too.

00:07:02.353 --> 00:07:07.120
Just have an automatic, temporary appropriations&nbsp;
bill pass when the deadline is missed.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:09.680
The opposing argument to&nbsp;
that is "Whoa, whoa, whoa."

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:13.600
"This is the one time we have every year to reset

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:17.680
and to kind of work things out and&nbsp;
so where would the incentive go?"

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:20.960
Congress has passed fewer&nbsp;
and fewer laws each year.

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:23.200
Passing these appropriations bills has become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:26.759
the one time they’re kind of&nbsp;
forced to agree on something.

00:07:26.759 --> 00:07:30.720
And as the political ideology of each&nbsp;
Congress grows further apart each year,

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:34.320
the likelihood of them agreeing&nbsp;
on time kind of goes down.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:35.930
So every fall...

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:37.680
"Washington’s version of groundhog day."

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:39.680
"Another government shutdown looms."

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:42.000
We are caught as pawns -

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.800
Pawns in an ultimate game as a federal employee&nbsp;
in a conversation that has nothing to do with us.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:50.800
We’re not going to work&nbsp;
increasing the profit of a CEO.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:55.083
We’re doing public services and now I’m&nbsp;
not going to get a paycheck? That’s crazy.

